Gfwrap Design Documents

Software Requirements Specification

1.	Core Algorithm: Huffman Coding
Requirement ID	Description	Technical Details
TR.1.1	Frequency Analysis	The compressor must perform a single pass read of the input image file to calculate the frequency of every 8-bit symbol (0-255). A fixed-size array/map of size 256 will be used for symbol counts.
TR.1.2	Tree Construction	Implement a Min-Priority Queue (using a C++ std::priority_queue or a custom binary heap) to build the Huffman tree based on calculated frequencies.
TR.1.3	Code Generation	A recursive tree traversal function (e.g., Depth-First Search) must generate the variable-length bit codes for every active symbol. Codes must be stored in a lookup table (e.g., std::map<uint8_t, std::vector<bool>> or a custom struct array).
TR.1.4	Bit-Level I/O	Implement a low-level BitReader and BitWriter utility to handle reading and writing individual bits for the compressed data stream. This is critical for achieving maximum compression ratio.

2.	Compressed File Format (.huff)
Requirement ID	Description	Technical Details
TR.2.1	Magic Number	The file must begin with a 4-byte magic number (e.g., 0x67667770 or "gfwp") to identify it as a gfwrap file and allow for basic error checking.
TR.2.2	Original Size	Store the original, uncompressed file size (in bytes) as an 8-byte (64-bit) unsigned integer immediately following the magic number.
TR.2.3	Huffman Tree Header	The serialized Huffman tree structure must be stored immediately before the compressed data. This header must be efficiently encoded (e.g., pre-order traversal sequence of leaf/node flags and symbol values) so the decompressor can rebuild the tree.
TR.2.4	Compressed Data	The bulk of the file must be the tightly packed bitstream, ending exactly at the point corresponding to the original file size. Any residual bits in the final byte must be handled via the original size (TR.2.2).

3.	Data Structures and Memory Management
Requirement ID	Description	Technical Details
TR.3.1	Modularity	Separate the application into distinct C++ classes/modules: HuffmanEncoder, HuffmanDecoder, BitStream, and CLIHandler.
TR.3.2	Huffman Node	Implement a HuffmanNode structure/class using smart pointers (std::unique_ptr or std::shared_ptr) for robust, automatic memory management of the tree structure. Avoid raw malloc/free for tree nodes.
TR.3.3	I/O Buffering	Implement internal buffers (e.g., 4KB or 8KB chunks) for both reading the input file and writing the compressed output to minimize system calls and improve I/O throughput.
TR.3.4	Error Handling	All data structure access (e.g., queue pop, pointer dereferencing) must be guarded against null/empty checks. Implement exception handling for critical failures (e.g., file I/O errors, invalid compressed format).


4.	Functional Requirements

Requirement ID	Description	Technical Details
TR.4.1	Compression	Input: Raw image file path. Output: .huff file. Function must implement steps TR.1.1 through TR.2.4.
TR.4.2	Decompression	Input: .huff file path. Output: Original image file path. Function must read the header (TR.2.3) to rebuild the tree, use the BitReader (TR.1.4) to decode the stream, and write exactly TR.2.2 bytes.
TR.4.3	CLI Interface (Draft)	The command line must support the following basic structure and flags: 


gfwrap <operation> <input_file> [options]
TR.4.4	Statistics Generation	A dedicated function must calculate and return the following metrics, triggered by a flag like -s or --stats: 


1. Compression Ratio: (Original Size / Compressed Size) 


2. Savings: (Original Size - Compressed Size) / Original Size * 100% 


3. Time: Elapsed time for compression/decompression (using <chrono>).

5.	Build and Environment
Requirement ID	Description	Technical Details
TR.5.1	Build System	Use CMake for build configuration. This ensures easy cross-platform compatibility (Linux/WSL). The project should define one target: gfwrap.
TR.5.2	Compiler Flags	Compile with flags necessary for high quality code: -Wall -Wextra -pedantic (maximum warnings) and -O2 (optimization) for the release build.
TR.5.3	Sanitization	Integrate Address Sanitizer (-fsanitize=address) and Undefined Behavior Sanitizer (-fsanitize=undefined) into a separate debug build target to rigorously enforce the memory discipline requirement.
TR.5.4	Source Organization	Place class declarations in header files (.h or .hpp) and implementation in source files (.cpp). A Utils directory for I/O and statistics code is recommended.


Software Architecure Diagram

 

1. System-Wide Concurrency Model: Single-Threaded with Asynchronous I/O Potential
Decision: For the initial version of gfwrap, a single-threaded, synchronous execution model is chosen. However, the design will anticipate and allow for future integration of asynchronous I/O without a major refactor.
Justification:
Simplicity & Debugging: For a command-line utility, introducing multi-threading from the outset adds significant complexity in terms of synchronization, race conditions, and debugging. Huffman coding itself is inherently sequential (frequency analysis then tree building then encoding).
Performance Bottlenecks: For image compression, the primary bottlenecks are usually CPU-bound (Huffman tree construction, bit manipulation) and I/O-bound (reading/writing large files). A single-threaded design allows us to isolate these and optimize them effectively first.
Anticipation for Asynchronous I/O (Future): The IOHandler and BitStream components will be designed with an interface that could later be adapted to use non-blocking I/O operations (e.g., io_uring on Linux, Boost.Asio) if profiling shows I/O to be a dominant bottleneck on very large files, or if a GUI wrapper is considered. This would allow the CPU to crunch data while the disk is spinning, but without the overhead of explicit user-managed threads.
No Shared State Complexity: Since there's no complex, shared mutable state between distinct, concurrent operations (e.g., one thread compressing while another decompresses the same file), a multi-threaded CPU-bound approach isn't immediately necessary.

2. Error Handling Model: Exception-Based for Critical, Sentinel-Based for Expected
Decision: A hybrid approach using C++ exceptions for unrecoverable or unexpected errors and sentinel values/status codes for expected, recoverable conditions.
Justification:
Exceptions for Critical Failures (e.g., File I/O):
File I/O Errors: If IOHandler cannot open a file, write to a full disk, or encounters corrupt file streams, these are unrecoverable failures for the current operation. Throwing exceptions (std::runtime_error, custom gfwrap_IOException) allows for immediate propagation up the call stack to gfwrap_App or CLIHandler for graceful termination and user notification.
Invalid File Format: If HuffmanDecoder detects an invalid magic number or a corrupted Huffman tree header, this is also a critical error. An exception here prevents further processing of invalid data.
Memory Allocation Failure (rare but critical): If new fails, std::bad_alloc will be thrown.
Sentinel Values / Status Codes for Expected Conditions:
CLI Argument Parsing: If the user provides invalid arguments (e.g., missing input file), the CLIHandler will return a status code or a boolean indicating failure, allowing gfwrap_App to print a usage message without crashing.
Empty Queue/Stack: Internal data structures (like the priority queue in Huffman tree building) might check for emptiness before popping; this is a logical condition handled by return values or assertions, not exceptions.
Benefits: This hybrid approach provides robust error detection without the performance overhead of exceptions for every minor fault. Exceptions ensure that critical, unhandled errors immediately terminate the affected operation cleanly, preventing silent data corruption or undefined behavior.

3. Data Flow Model: Stream-Oriented, Pipelined Processing
Decision: Data flow will be primarily stream-oriented and pipelined, minimizing large in-memory buffers where possible, especially for the core compression/decompression algorithms.
Justification:
Memory Efficiency: For potentially very large image files (Non-functional: "Efficient memory management"), processing data as a stream avoids loading the entire file into RAM, which is crucial for a "lightweight" utility.
Compression:
1.	Pass 1 (Frequency Analysis): IOHandler reads the input file in buffered chunks. HuffmanCompressor updates symbol frequencies. No full file in memory.
2.	Pass 2 (Encoding): IOHandler reads the input file again (or seeks back to start if supported), HuffmanCompressor uses the BitStream to read byte-by-byte, encode bit-by-bit, and BitStream writes the compressed bits to IOHandler, which then writes to the output file in buffered chunks.
Decompression: IOHandler reads the compressed file in buffered chunks. BitStream reads bits. HuffmanDecoder decodes bits into symbols. IOHandler writes decompressed symbols to the output file in buffered chunks.
Clear Component Responsibilities:
IOHandler: Solely responsible for interacting with the OS file system (opening, reading bytes, writing bytes).
BitStream: Handles the byte-to-bit and bit-to-byte conversion, buffering, and management of the underlying byte stream provided by IOHandler. This decouples bit-level logic from raw file I/O.
HuffmanCompressor/HuffmanDecoder: Focuses purely on the Huffman algorithm, interacting with BitStream for data input/output.
Flexibility: This stream-oriented design makes it easier to adapt the utility to handle pipes (stdin/stdout) in the future, increasing its "Linux utility" feel, without a major internal re-architecture.

