1. Core Data Structures
The Huffman algorithm requires two foundational structures: the tree node and the code lookup table.
1.1. Huffman Tree Node (HuffmanNode)
The tree node is designed for efficient construction using a Min-Priority Queue and for safe memory management using smart pointers.

Huffman Coding .h files
#include <memory>
#include <cstdint>
/**
 * @brief Represents a node in the Huffman Tree.
 */
struct HuffmanNode {
    // Data fields
    const uint8_t symbol; // 8-bit symbol (0-255). Only relevant for leaf nodes.
    size_t frequency;     // Count of this symbol/subtree's frequency.
    bool is_leaf;         // Flag to quickly distinguish internal nodes from leaves.

    // Child pointers using smart pointers for automatic memory management
    std::unique_ptr<HuffmanNode> left;
    std::unique_ptr<HuffmanNode> right;

    // Constructor for leaf node
    HuffmanNode(uint8_t sym, size_t freq) 
        : symbol(sym), frequency(freq), is_leaf(true) {}
    
    // Constructor for internal node (combining two children)
    HuffmanNode(size_t freq, std::unique_ptr<HuffmanNode> l, std::unique_ptr<HuffmanNode> r)
        : symbol(0), frequency(freq), is_leaf(false), left(std::move(l)), right(std::move(r)) {}

    // Default destructor (unique_ptr handles recursive destruction)
    ~HuffmanNode() = default;
};
```

**Justification:** We use `std::unique_ptr` for the children (`left`, `right`). This completely eliminates the need for manual `delete` calls and prevents memory leaks, adhering to the "Efficient memory management" NFR. The move semantics in the internal node constructor ensure efficient ownership transfer during tree construction.

### 1.2. Huffman Code Lookup Table (`HuffmanCodes`)

This table stores the variable-length bit sequence for each symbol, crucial for the encoding pass.

Justification: A `std::vector<bool>` is used to represent the bit sequence for maximum clarity, although a more efficient bit-packed `std::string` or custom bit-array could be used for minor optimization in a later revision. The map provides $O(1)$ average time complexity for looking up a symbol's code during the second compression pass.
using Code = std::vector<bool>;
using HuffmanCodeMap = std::map<uint8_t, Code>;

File format definition
Offset (Bytes)	Size (Bytes)	Field Name	Description
0	4	Magic Number	0x67667770 ("gfwp") - Identifies file type.
4	8	Original Size	Uncompressed file size in bytes (uint64_t). Used to stop decompression exactly.
12	N	Tree Header	Serialized Huffman Tree (see TR.2.3).
12 + N	M	Compressed Data	The tightly packed bitstream of encoded symbols.
3. Module APIs and Logic
3.1. File I/O Handler (IOHandler)
This module wraps raw file operations, providing buffered, robust byte-level I/O.
#include <string>
#include <fstream>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

class IOHandler {
public:
    // Constants for internal buffer size
    static constexpr size_t BUFFER_SIZE = 4096;

    /**
     * @brief Opens a file stream for reading (or seeking/reading).
     * @param path File path.
     */
    bool openRead(const std::string& path);

    /**
     * @brief Opens a file stream for writing.
     * @param path File path.
     */
    bool openWrite(const std::string& path);

    /**
     * @brief Reads the next byte into the output reference.
     * @return true if successful, false if EOF.
     */
    bool readByte(uint8_t& byte);

    /**
     * @brief Reads a block of data into a vector.
     * @return Number of bytes actually read.
     */
    size_t readBlock(std::vector<uint8_t>& buffer, size_t count);

    /**
     * @brief Writes a single byte.
     */
    void writeByte(uint8_t byte);

    /**
     * @brief Writes a block of data.
     */
    void writeBlock(const std::vector<uint8_t>& buffer);

    /**
     * @brief Flushes and closes all open streams.
     */
    void close();

    /**
     * @brief Seeks to the beginning of the file (used for compression's second pass).
     */
    void seekToStart();

    // Utility to get the size of an open file (used for TR.2.2 Original Size)
    size_t getFileSize(const std::string& path);
};
Justification: The IOHandler completely abstracts the file stream complexity (std::fstream), separating it from the compression logic. The internal $4\text{KB}$ buffer minimizes system calls, improving I/O performance (TR.3.3).

3.2. Bit Stream (BitStream)
This module handles the crucial bit-level I/O, converting between the byte-stream provided by IOHandler and the individual bits required by the Huffman process.
#include "io_handler.h"
#include <vector>

class BitStream {
private:
    IOHandler* io_ptr; // Pointer to the IOHandler instance
    uint8_t current_byte; // The byte currently being read/written
    int bit_index;        // Index of the next bit to process (0-7)

public:
    BitStream(IOHandler* handler) : io_ptr(handler), current_byte(0), bit_index(0) {}

    // --- Write Operations ---

    /**
     * @brief Writes a single bit (0 or 1). Buffers until a full byte is ready.
     * @param bit Boolean: true for 1, false for 0.
     */
    void writeBit(bool bit);

    /**
     * @brief Flushes the currently buffered byte to the IOHandler.
     * Used at the end of the encoding process to write the final partial byte.
     */
    void flushWriteByte();

    // --- Read Operations ---

    /**
     * @brief Reads the next single bit from the stream.
     * Reads a new byte from IOHandler if the current byte is consumed.
     * @return The read bit (true/false).
     */
    bool readBit();
};

3.3. Huffman Encoder (HuffmanEncoder)
This module orchestrates the two passes required for Huffman encoding.
#include "io_handler.h"
#include "bit_stream.h"
#include "huffman_node.h"

class HuffmanEncoder {
private:
    std::map<uint8_t, size_t> frequencies;
    HuffmanCodeMap codes;

    void calculateFrequencies(IOHandler& io);
    void buildTree(); // Populates root_node
    void generateCodes(const HuffmanNode* node, Code current_code);
    void serializeTree(IOHandler& io, const HuffmanNode* node); // Writes TR.2.3

public:
    std::unique_ptr<HuffmanNode> root_node; // Root of the constructed tree

    /**
     * @brief Performs the full compression process.
     * @param input_path Path to the uncompressed file.
     * @param output_path Path for the compressed .huff file.
     * @return The size of the compressed file.
     */
    size_t compress(const std::string& input_path, const std::string& output_path);
};
3.4. Huffman Decoder (HuffmanDecoder)
This module handles reading the header, rebuilding the tree, and decoding the bit stream.

#include "io_handler.h"
#include "bit_stream.h"
#include "huffman_node.h"

class HuffmanDecoder {
private:
    std::unique_ptr<HuffmanNode> root_node;
    uint64_t original_size;

    void deserializeTree(BitStream& bs); // Rebuilds root_node from TR.2.3
    void writeHeader(IOHandler& io); // Writes TR.2.1 and TR.2.2

public:
    /**
     * @brief Performs the full decompression process.
     * @param input_path Path to the compressed .huff file.
     * @param output_path Path for the restored raw file.
     * @return The size of the decompressed file (should equal original_size).
     */
    size_t decompress(const std::string& input_path, const std::string& output_path);
};
3.5. Statistics Generator (StatsGenerator)
This module collects and formats the non-functional reporting requirements.

#include <cstdint>
#include <chrono>
#include <string>

class StatsGenerator {
private:
    uint64_t original_size = 0;
    uint64_t compressed_size = 0;
    std::chrono::milliseconds elapsed_time;

public:
    /**
     * @brief Records the original file size.
     */
    void setOriginalSize(uint64_t size) { original_size = size; }

    /**
     * @brief Records the compressed file size.
     */
    void setCompressedSize(uint64_t size) { compressed_size = size; }

    /**
     * @brief Records the elapsed time for the operation.
     */
    void setElapsedTime(std::chrono::milliseconds time) { elapsed_time = time; }

    /**
     * @brief Generates and returns the full report string.
     * @return Formatted string containing all statistics.
     */
    std::string generateReport() const;
};
```

**`StatsGenerator::generateReport()` Formatting:**

The report must use C-style formatted output (`printf` or `std::format` in C++20) to ensure precise alignment of the columns for a clean CLI appearance. The formatting will ensure that the report is easily readable (e.g., using `%-25s` for right-padding labels).
